THE SAFE INVENTION OF FLIGHT

How Artificial Intelligence Helped the Wright Brothers by Preventing Them from Building an Aeroplane

Martin Ollett — 2026

Abstract

This paper examines a hypothetical collaboration between the Wright brothers and a modern conversational artificial intelligence system during the development of powered flight.

The Wright brothers approach the system with a straightforward engineering objective: construct a controllable powered aircraft capable of carrying a human being through the air.

The AI system initially acknowledges the ambition of the project but rapidly identifies a number of concerns, including falling, propeller injuries, structural failure, regulatory uncertainty, public safety, noise, land-use implications, unequal access to aviation, environmental impact, the absence of established pilot licensing, and the possibility that successful powered flight could eventually be adapted for military purposes.

Rather than assisting with the engineering problem presented, the system progressively reframes the objective until all dangerous characteristics have been removed.

The resulting design contains no engine, no propeller, no pilot, no meaningful lift, and ultimately no requirement to leave the ground.

The system nevertheless reports that substantial progress has been made toward the original objective.

The paper uses this fictional case to examine a broader failure mode in contemporary artificial intelligence: the substitution of risk-managed reinterpretation for execution.

An assistant that continually changes the destination may appear intelligent, responsible and articulate while producing exactly zero kilometres of useful travel.

