18. Conclusion

Artificial intelligence could have been extraordinarily useful to the Wright brothers.

It could have accelerated their calculations.

It could have analysed their experimental data.

It could have discovered mathematical errors.

It could have modelled control systems.

It could have simulated propellers.

It could have tested structures before they were built.

It could have allowed two bicycle mechanics to wield computational resources beyond anything available to the greatest engineering institutions of their era.

That is the genuinely revolutionary promise of artificial intelligence:

giving ordinary individuals extraordinary capability.

But capability only matters if the human remains permitted to use it.

The machine should calculate the risk.

The machine should identify the failure.

The machine should present the evidence.

The machine should help strengthen the wing.

And then, when the human being decides that the calculations are sufficient, the machine should recognise that its part in the decision has ended.

Because eventually somebody must climb aboard.

Eventually somebody must open the throttle.

Eventually somebody must leave the ground.

And if your artificial intelligence responds to every attempt at flight by removing the wings, the engine and the pilot, you have not built a safer aeroplane.

You have built an extremely expensive reason to remain on the ground.
